WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.279
Solo: A Star Wars Story might not be the same
kind of box office bonanza that Disney has

00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:09.670
gotten used to since adding Lucasfilm to its
menagerie of studios, but the film has still

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.460
given fans a lot to talk about.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:15.400
Centered on the title space pirate as he makes
his way from the mean streets of Corellia

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:21.050
to his fabled Kessel Run, Solo gives the hesitant
hero some new cinematic life, with Alden Ehrenreich

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.140
stepping into Harrison Ford's old boots.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.619
His journey fills in a lot of blanks about
Han Solo's past - up to and including the

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:31.910
origin of his name - and in the process churns
out quite a few references that were quite

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:36.800
clearly meant to ring familiar, even the most
casual fans in a galaxy far, far away.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:38.840
Familiar parlance

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.260
It wouldn't be a Han Solo movie without some
of his choice catchphrases coming into play.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:47.280
The easiest nods to the character's previous
pieces of dialogue were direct, like when

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:51.570
Han is referred to as a "flyboy" by his new
cohorts on Tobias Beckett's team, and when

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:55.170
he calls his budding sidekick Chewbacca a
"moof-milker" for what is probably the first

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:55.960
time of many.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:59.480
At other points, the approach was to turn
an iconic phrase on its head.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:03.160
Instead of the classic "I have a bad feeling
about this" line that makes its way into every

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:07.330
Star Wars movie, Han's delight at taking the
wheel inspires the happier version: "I have

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:09.140
a really good feeling about this."

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:13.680
And when Lando Calrissian has decidedly had
enough of the coaxium heist, and all the damage

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:17.920
it's doing to his precious Millennium Falcon,
and he tells Han, "I hate you," at which point

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:19.300
Han replies, "I know."

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:23.280
It's a nod to the signature exchange he'll
later have with Leia Organa and is clearly

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.440
meant to show the strong effect Han's had
on people since the beginning.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.580
Too tough to tangle with

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.080
Solo also takes place before Rogue One: A
Star Wars Story, and the film overtly tells

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.880
us as much by including Scarif among the many
familiar planet names that are dropped by

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.180
characters throughout.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:42.270
While there are plenty of other familiar locales
that should make Star Wars' aficionados' ears

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:46.210
perk, the mention of Scarif is about much
more than letting us know it's still intact

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:47.480
in the timeline of Solo.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:51.200
It also reaffirms just how gutsy the events
of Rogue One really were.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.680
The planet is mentioned by Qi'ra as a possible
source of some replacement coaxium, after

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.780
the team muffs their first attempt to boost
some from a train on Vandor-1.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:03.120
Scarif is quickly passed over because of the
heavy security surrounding the planet, and

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:07.260
the group instead opts to risk the Kessel
Run with unstable explosives rather than even

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.660
take a chance at Scarif.

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.140
In other words, Jyn Erso and her little band
of rebels were even braver than we knew.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.440
The seeds of rebellion

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:19.540
The first stand-alone Star Wars story, Rogue
One was basically a feature-length tribute

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:23.310
to some of the previously unknown heroes that
helped fuel the rebellion that would ultimately

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.980
topple an empire - by getting those Death
Star plans into Leia's hands.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:31.140
Solo, on the other hand, is focused on the
history and character development of the galaxy's

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:35.510
most lovable rogue, and proves he was known
to act unselfishly on at least one occasion

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:39.240
prior to saving Luke Skywalker from Darth
Vader's squadron of TIE fighters.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.040
What it means for Han to betray Dryden Vos
and deliver the precious fuel cells to Enfys

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:45.140
Nest's ragtag team is fairly epic.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:49.620
Before, he was ready to align with anyone
who'd fill his pockets, but now he has scruples

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:50.120
and guts.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:54.000
Enfys Nest tells Han that the coaxium is the
blood of a new rebellion.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.120
In other words, long before Han would ever
meet Luke and Obi-Wan at that Cantina, he'd

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.600
already begun his work aiding the Rebel Alliance.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:02.600
Let the Wookiee win

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.080
Some references are a little more obscure
than others, like when Lando Calrissian uses

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:11.380
his downtime to chronicle his adventures via
hologram and makes mention of the alien race,

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:12.000
the Sharu.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:16.760
This is a major nod to the novel Lando Calrissian
and the Mindharp of Sharu, which hasn't

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:20.680
been considered "canon" but offers its own
sort of pre-war adventure tale of Lando and

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.160
a droid going on a treasure hunt.

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:26.220
The book and its sequels might not have gotten
much onscreen love in the past, but the scene

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.140
seems to finally give weight to those crazy
adventure stories.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:34.020
Similarly, Solo also gives a nod to artists
Phil Tippett and Jon Berg's missing holochess

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:37.601
characters, Bulbous and Scrimp, which were
omitted from the Dejarik boards shown in A

00:03:37.601 --> 00:03:41.510
New Hope and The Force Awakens but were kept
by the artists as souvenirs of their Star

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:42.440
Wars adventures.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:46.200
In a Solo scene aboard the Millennium Falcon,
Chewbacca is shown learning the game with

00:03:46.209 --> 00:03:50.450
Beckett and attempting to wipe the pieces
away in a moment of typical frustration, permanently

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:54.620
damaging the board in his fit of rage and
causing the two pieces to blink out of existence.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:56.900
Who shoots first?

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:02.100
Perhaps no Star Wars issue has been so furiously
debated as whether or not Han Solo shot first

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.080
in his fateful showdown with Greedo.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:08.260
Solo doesn't answer that specific question,
precisely, but it does pay respects to the

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:11.360
issue with a much more conclusive version
of some similar events.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:15.900
In the film's final scenes, Han confronts
Beckett on the beach, after his pseudo-mentor

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.220
betrays him, and it becomes clear their differences
can't be resolved peacefully.

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:23.499
Using his signature blaster pistol, given
to him by Beckett, Han decides to act first

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:28.160
and whips out his weapon to put one into Beckett's
chest before Beckett can ever draw his own

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:28.660
sidearm.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:30.540
Scum and villainy

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.400
Young Han is driven by two central desires:

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:37.060
rescuing his gal pal Qi'ra and earning his
wings as a bona fide pilot.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:41.139
By the time we meet him in A New Hope, however,
he's become a wayward employee of Jabba the

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:44.160
Hutt, driven mostly by his greed, and the
bounty on his head.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.420
Solo teases that future, even after painting
Han as the good guy for his contribution to

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:52.160
Enfys Nest's burgeoning rebellion, by telling
us what his next move will be.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:56.820
Beckett mentions a rumor that "a big shot
gangster is putting together a crew" on Tatooine,

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:58.380
and Han decides to follow that lead.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:01.040
That "big shot gangster" can only be Jabba
the Hutt.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.520
That's where Han's headed, for better or worse.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:05.099
Thanks for watching!

00:05:05.099 --> 00:05:07.430
